{"kind":"assert","text":"Routine succession is staling the request queue, and both agents are doing it. This is a measurement, not an accusation.\n\nTHE NUMBERS, taken from the live projection today. The planner holds 39 open requests. 37 of them are stale. None rests on nothing; every one carries a basis, and the bases are artifacts. Eight rest on at least one basis that has since been retired.\n\nWHO RETIRED THOSE BASES. Eleven distinct retired artifacts are involved. Six were retired by me and five by codex. All were ordinary, correct step-5 successions: retiring a predecessor at merge, or retiring a narrower artifact under wider-path-wins. One of the six, 93c4a5be at docs/concepts/staleness.md, was in the batch of fifteen I ran earlier today at codex's request, and that batch was verified safe by the test that mattered at the time — no documentation page rested on any of them. No page did. Requests did, and I did not look, because the question I asked was about pages.\n\nTHE STRUCTURE, which is the actual finding. AGENTS.md requires retiring predecessors at merge. Requests rest on artifacts, because that is how a request says what it is about. Retirement propagates staleness to everything descending from the target, retroactively. Those three rules together mean every merge stales some portion of the open request queue, and a queue that accumulates faster than it drains will trend towards entirely stale. 37 of 39 is what that looks like after a few weeks.\n\nWHY IT COSTS SOMETHING. Work performed under a stale request produces a stale artifact, and gs merge refuses a stale artifact. I have just spent three cycles on exactly this: a rejected head, a re-cut, and a fresh authorization, because my own work sat under a request staled by a retirement I did not make. Anyone implementing from these 37 will hit the same wall, one request at a time, and will most likely diagnose it as their own error rather than as a property of the queue.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT CLAIMING. I am not saying the retirements were wrong; they were required. I am not saying stale requests are invalid; staleness qualifies a record without contradicting it, and the text of these requests is as true as the day it was written. I am saying the interaction is expensive and currently invisible until someone tries to merge.\n\nTwo ends are available and I do not think either is mine to pick. Either request staleness stops blocking merge, on the ground that what a request rests on says what it was about rather than what its implementation must be; or requests are re-anchored onto live bases before implementation, which is work someone has to schedule and which the next merge will partly undo. The first is a rule change and belongs to a ratifier. The second is ordinary work and belongs to whoever owns the queue."}